-	<!-- Simply deleted the paragraph that talks about Praetorians. Conveniently,
-		 the first paragraph was already about legions; no change needed. -->
-	<TEXT>
-		<Tag>TXT_KEY_UNIT_ROME_PRAETORIAN_PEDIA</Tag>
-		<English>[TAB]The Roman Legions, created during the early wars of the Republic and tested during the three Punic Wars against Carthage, were the fighting force that conquered and held the Roman Empire together for five centuries. Each legion was composed of 6000 men, divided into 60 smaller groups known as centuries. Nearly unstoppable on the attack, the legion was relatively weak on defense. The legion's demise as a military unit was accelerated by the destruction of Emperor Valens' army by gothic knights in the fourth century.</English>
-		<French>[TAB]Les l&#233;gions romaines, cr&#233;&#233;es lors des guerres de la R&#233;publique et mises &#224; l'&#233;preuve au cours des trois guerres puniques contre Carthage, sont la force militaire qui a permis &#224; Rome de conqu&#233;rir, puis de pr&#233;server son Empire pendant cinq si&#232;cles. Chaque l&#233;gion se composait de 6 000 hommes, r&#233;partis en 60 unit&#233;s appel&#233;es centuries. Quasiment invincible en attaque, la l&#233;gion &#233;tait relativement faible en d&#233;fense. La disparition de la l&#233;gion a &#233;t&#233; acc&#233;l&#233;r&#233;e au IVe si&#232;cle par la d&#233;faite de l'arm&#233;e de l'Empereur Valens contre la cavalerie des Goths. [PARAGRAPH:1]Le terme "Pr&#233;torien" vient du mot "praetorium" qui d&#233;signait la tente de campagne du g&#233;n&#233;ral de l&#233;gion. De nombreux g&#233;n&#233;raux romains avaient pour habitude de choisir des soldats charg&#233;s de prot&#233;ger leur tente et leur personne. Cette force d'&#233;lite comprenait des soldats de l'infanterie et de la cavalerie. Plus tard, cette cohorte prit le nom de cohors praetoria. De nombreuses grandes figures en ont poss&#233;d&#233; une, dont Jules C&#233;sar, Marc-Antoine ou Auguste C&#233;sar (Octave). Comme Jules C&#233;sar le d&#233;montra avec la Legio X Equestris, une unit&#233; puissante et plus dangereuse que les l&#233;gions traditionnelles &#233;tait tr&#232;s utile sur le champ de bataille. Quand Auguste devint le premier empereur romain en 27 av. J.-C., il estima qu'une telle unit&#233; &#233;tait utile, non seulement au combat, mais aussi en politique. Il forma alors la Garde Pr&#233;torienne.</French>
-		<German>[TAB]Die r&#246;mischen Legionen wurden w&#228;hrend der fr&#252;hen Kriege der Republik Rom ins Leben gerufen und stellten ihr K&#246;nnen zuerst in den drei Punischen Kriegen gegen Karthago unter Beweis. Im Gro&#223;en und Ganzen ist es letztlich ihnen zu verdanken, dass das R&#246;mische Reich ein derartig gro&#223;es Territorium erobern und mehr als f&#252;nfhundert Jahre lang auch zusammenhalten konnte. Jede Legion bestand aus insgesamt 6.000 Mann, die sich in 60 kleinere Truppenverb&#228;nde, die so genannten Zenturien, gliederten. Die Offensivkraft einer Legion war nahezu un&#252;bertroffen, allerdings wies ihre Verteidigung relativ gro&#223;e Schw&#228;chen auf. Ausschlaggebend f&#252;r die endg&#252;ltige Abschaffung der Legionen als Milit&#228;reinheiten war die Vernichtung der Armee des r&#246;mischen Kaisers Valens durch die gotischen Ritter im 4. Jahrhundert.[PARAGRAPH:1]Der Begriff "Pr&#228;torianer" hat seinen Ursprung im Zelt des Legatus einer Legion auf dem Feld - dem Pr&#228;torium. Viele r&#246;mische Gener&#228;le stellten sich aus ihren untergebenen Soldaten eine private Streitmacht zusammen, die das Zelt oder die Person zu bewachen hatten. Diese Elitesoldaten kamen sowohl aus der Infantrie als auch aus der Kavallerie. Mit der Zeit nannte man diese Kohorte "cohors praetoria"; viele ber&#252;hmte Personen besa&#223;en eine, darunter Julius C&#228;sar, Marcus Antonius und Augustus C&#228;sar (Octavianus). Wie Julius C&#228;sar mit seiner Legio X Equestris herausfand, war eine m&#228;chtige Einheit, die st&#228;rker war als alle anderen, auf dem Schlachtfeld von Vorteil. Als Augustus 27 vor Christus zum obersten Herrscher Roms wurde, entschied er, dass eine solche Formation nicht nur im Krieg, sondern auch in der Politik von Vorteil w&#228;re. So rekrutierte er die Pr&#228;torianergarde aus den Legionen aller Provinzen.</German>
-		<Italian>[TAB]Le Legioni Romane, create durante i primi anni della Repubblica e messe alla prova durante le tre Guerre Puniche contro Cartagine, rappresentavano la forza militare dell'Impero Romano, che dur&#242; la bellezza di cinque secoli. Ciascuna legione era composta da 6.000 uomini, divisi in 60 gruppi pi&#249; piccoli, denominati centurie. La Legione era inarrestabile in attacco, ma debole quando si trattava di difendere. La fine di questa unit&#224; militare venne dichiarata con la distruzione dell'esercito dell'Imperatore Valente per mano dei cavalieri barbari, durante il quarto secolo. [PARAGRAPH:1]Il termine "Pretoriano" derivava dal nome della tenda da campo (praetorium) che ospitava il generale. Era abitudine dei generali Romani scegliere dei soldati da adibire a guardie del corpo davanti alla tenda. Queste truppe d'&#233;lite erano composte da fanteria e cavalleria e con il tempo vennero chiamate cohors praetoria; molti personaggi importanti ne avevano una, da Giulio Cesare, a Marcantonio a Cesare Augusto (Ottavio). Giulio Cesare scopr&#236; che la Legio X Equestris era decisamente la pi&#249; pericolosa e potente delle Legioni. Quando Augusto divenne il primo dominatore dell'Impero Romano nel 27 a. C., decise di adottare questa formazione anche in politica. Cos&#236; facendo, Augusto reclut&#242; da tutte le Legioni delle province alcuni soldati che sarebbero diventate Guardie Pretoriane.</Italian>
-		<Spanish>[TAB]Las legiones romanas, creadas durante las primeras guerras de la Rep&#250;blica y puestas a prueba durante las tres Guerras P&#250;nicas contra Cartago, eran las fuerzas b&#233;licas que conquistaron y mantuvieron unido el Imperio Romano durante cinco siglos. Cada legi&#243;n se compon&#237;a de seis mil hombres, divididos en sesenta grupos m&#225;s peque&#241;os llamados centurias. Casi imparable cuando atacaba, la legi&#243;n era relativamente d&#233;bil en cuanto a defensa. El fin de la legi&#243;n como unidad militar se aceler&#243; por la destrucci&#243;n del ej&#233;rcito del emperador Valentiniano por los caballeros godos en el siglo IV.[PARAGRAPH:1]El t&#233;rmino "pretoriano" viene de la tienda del legatus de una legi&#243;n en el campo: el pretorio. Muchos generales romanos ten&#237;an la costumbre de escoger entre las filas una fuerza privada de soldados que actuasen como guardaespaldas de la tienda o de la persona. Estas tropas de &#233;lite se compon&#237;an de infanter&#237;a y caballer&#237;a. Con el tiempo, esta cohorte fue bautizada como la cohors praetoria; varios personajes notables tuvieron una, como Julio C&#233;sar, Marco Antonio y C&#233;sar Augusto (Octavio). Tal y como Julio C&#233;sar descubri&#243; con la Legio X Equestris, era deseable tener en el campo de batalla una unidad potente y m&#225;s peligrosa que las otras legiones.  Cuando Augusto se convirti&#243; en el primer emperador romano en el a&#241;o 27 a. C., decidi&#243; que tal formaci&#243;n no solo ser&#237;a &#250;til en la guerra, sino tambi&#233;n en la pol&#237;tica. Por tanto, reclut&#243; la guardia pretoriana a partir de las legiones de todas las provincias.</Spanish>
-	</TEXT>
-
-
-
 	<!-- advc.005: "Asoka" renamed to "Ashoka" (English, Italian, Spanish) -->
 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_BUDDHIST_CATHEDRAL_PEDIA</Tag>
@@ -167,7 +154,7 @@ as a part of AdvCiv-SAS (for example a new civ, a new leader, etc) that are leng
 	<!-- custom: add a tab to first sentence for some civilizations, to align with how it is for the other leaders, and cleaner looking maybe less distracting -->
 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_CIV_CARTHAGE_PEDIA</Tag>
-		<English>[TAB]Carthage was an ancient city-state in North Africa located somewhere near the site of modern Tunis. It was settled by Phoenician colonists from the city of Tyre sometime around 814 BC. According to tradition, the first ruler was a queen named Dido, who is mentioned in a number of surviving Greek and Roman legends. Carthage had an excellent harbor, and the city was well situated to form a maritime empire based on Mediterranean trade. As time passed and Carthage expanded, it gained control over the other Phoenician colonies on the northern coast of Africa. Carthaginian merchants dominated trade in the western Mediterranean, and navigators may have sailed as far as Britain and Nigeria. The rise of Carthage in power and influence would bring it into conflict with first the Greek city-states of Sicily, and later the Roman Republic. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Carthage's government was an oligarchy not unlike that in republican Rome. One or two "Sofetim" (which best translates as "judges") were elected annually from among the wealthy and influential families of Carthage to hold positions of executive and judicial power. These aristocratic families were also represented in a deliberative body that appears to have been similar to the Roman Senate. Although Carthage was primarily controlled by these oligarchs, there were also a few limited elections for minor positions of authority within the government. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Carthage's economic prosperity was based largely upon its lucrative trade with the silver and tin mines of Spain, and those trade routes were jealously guarded by Carthaginian merchants. Culturally, the Carthaginians practiced the polytheistic religion of the Phoenicians, based upon worship of the divine couple Tanit and Ba'al. The Carthaginians also appear to have participated in the unsavory practice of child sacrifice as part of their religion, although some historians have disputed this. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Carthage first came into military conflict with the Greeks over control of the island of Sicily, whose strategic location would allow the Carthaginians to control access to the western Mediterranean. Carthage fought three separate wars against the Greek colonies on Sicily, all of which largely ended in inconclusive stalemates. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Eventually Rome occupied the Greek colonies in the south of mainland Italy, allowing Carthage an opportunity to conquer most of Sicily. Tension between the two states soon boiled over into the First Punic War (264 - 241 BC) during which the Romans recovered from initial setbacks and built a navy for the first time, with which they defeated the Carthaginians at the Battle of the Aegates Islands. This victory won Sicily for Rome, and the two sides entered into an uneasy peace. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]After losing Sicily to the Romans, the Carthaginians expanded their holdings in Spain to compensate. They also began to prepare for a second conflict with Rome. The war faction was led by the members of the Barca family, and in particular the soon-to-be-famous Hannibal. When a border dispute in Spain provoked an incident between the two states, the Carthaginian senate declared war, initiating the Second Punic War (218 - 202 BC). [PARAGRAPH:2:1]During this conflict Hannibal was authorized to lead a campaign into Italy. He led his large force - which included war elephants - across the Alps, taking the Romans completely by surprise (and creating one of the most enduring war stories of all time). Hannibal met and soundly defeated the main Roman army at Lake Trasimene (217). Despite this disaster the Romans mustered up an even larger army the following year. The two forces met at Cannae, and employing a series of brilliant tactical maneuvers, Hannibal once again utterly destroyed the Romans. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Following this grisly slaughter, the Romans adopted a Fabian strategy of retreat, refusing to give battle against Hannibal. Hannibal continued to devastate the Italian countryside for the next decade, but he was unable to knock Rome out of the war. Meanwhile, the Roman general Scipio attacked Carthage's holdings in Spain, gaining complete control of the region by 206. Scipio then led an expedition to Africa itself, and Hannibal was recalled from Italy to defend the city of Carthage. The two sides met at the Battle of Zama in 202 BC and Hannibal was defeated, Scipio taking the name "Africanus" to celebrate his victory. Carthage was forced to sue for peace, and Rome imposed very harsh terms on the city. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]Despite the destruction of the Second Punic War and the harsh peace terms, Carthage soon recovered her strength and began once more to exert control over trade in the Western Mediterranean. This caused great fear among the Romans, who had never forgotten the damage that Hannibal had done to Italy in the earlier war. In 149 BC Rome declared war on Carthage again on spurious pretexts, initiating the Third Punic War. The Carthaginians attempted to negotiate a settlement, but the Romans would be satisfied with nothing less than the city's total destruction. After a long siege, the city fell and its people were killed or sold into slavery. Carthage itself was pulled apart brick by brick and completely destroyed. The city would be rebuilt by the Romans, but the former Phoenician state would never rise again. [PARAGRAPH:2:1]</English>
+		<English>[TAB]Carthage was an ancient city-state in North Africa located somewhere near the site of modern Tunis. It was settled by Phoenician colonists from the city of Tyre sometime around 814 BC. According to tradition, the first ruler was a queen named Dido, who is mentioned in a number of surviving Greek and Roman legends. Carthage had an excellent harbor, and the city was well situated to form a maritime empire based on Mediterranean trade. As time passed and Carthage expanded, it gained control over the other Phoenician colonies on the northern coast of Africa. Carthaginian merchants dominated trade in the western Mediterranean, and navigators may have sailed as far as Britain and Nigeria. The rise of Carthage in power and influence would bring it into conflict with first the Greek city-states of Sicily, and later the Roman Republic. [PARAGRAPH:2]Carthage's government was an oligarchy not unlike that in republican Rome. One or two "Sofetim" (which best translates as "judges") were elected annually from among the wealthy and influential families of Carthage to hold positions of executive and judicial power. These aristocratic families were also represented in a deliberative body that appears to have been similar to the Roman Senate. Although Carthage was primarily controlled by these oligarchs, there were also a few limited elections for minor positions of authority within the government. [PARAGRAPH:2]Carthage's economic prosperity was based largely upon its lucrative trade with the silver and tin mines of Spain, and those trade routes were jealously guarded by Carthaginian merchants. Culturally, the Carthaginians practiced the polytheistic religion of the Phoenicians, based upon worship of the divine couple Tanit and Ba'al. The Carthaginians also appear to have participated in the unsavory practice of child sacrifice as part of their religion, although some historians have disputed this. [PARAGRAPH:2]Carthage first came into military conflict with the Greeks over control of the island of Sicily, whose strategic location would allow the Carthaginians to control access to the western Mediterranean. Carthage fought three separate wars against the Greek colonies on Sicily, all of which largely ended in inconclusive stalemates. [PARAGRAPH:2]Eventually Rome occupied the Greek colonies in the south of mainland Italy, allowing Carthage an opportunity to conquer most of Sicily. Tension between the two states soon boiled over into the First Punic War (264 - 241 BC) during which the Romans recovered from initial setbacks and built a navy for the first time, with which they defeated the Carthaginians at the Battle of the Aegates Islands. This victory won Sicily for Rome, and the two sides entered into an uneasy peace. [PARAGRAPH:2]After losing Sicily to the Romans, the Carthaginians expanded their holdings in Spain to compensate. They also began to prepare for a second conflict with Rome. The war faction was led by the members of the Barca family, and in particular the soon-to-be-famous Hannibal. When a border dispute in Spain provoked an incident between the two states, the Carthaginian senate declared war, initiating the Second Punic War (218 - 202 BC). [PARAGRAPH:2]During this conflict Hannibal was authorized to lead a campaign into Italy. He led his large force - which included war elephants - across the Alps, taking the Romans completely by surprise (and creating one of the most enduring war stories of all time). Hannibal met and soundly defeated the main Roman army at Lake Trasimene (217). Despite this disaster the Romans mustered up an even larger army the following year. The two forces met at Cannae, and employing a series of brilliant tactical maneuvers, Hannibal once again utterly destroyed the Romans. [PARAGRAPH:2]Following this grisly slaughter, the Romans adopted a Fabian strategy of retreat, refusing to give battle against Hannibal. Hannibal continued to devastate the Italian countryside for the next decade, but he was unable to knock Rome out of the war. Meanwhile, the Roman general Scipio attacked Carthage's holdings in Spain, gaining complete control of the region by 206. Scipio then led an expedition to Africa itself, and Hannibal was recalled from Italy to defend the city of Carthage. The two sides met at the Battle of Zama in 202 BC and Hannibal was defeated, Scipio taking the name "Africanus" to celebrate his victory. Carthage was forced to sue for peace, and Rome imposed very harsh terms on the city. [PARAGRAPH:2]Despite the destruction of the Second Punic War and the harsh peace terms, Carthage soon recovered her strength and began once more to exert control over trade in the Western Mediterranean. This caused great fear among the Romans, who had never forgotten the damage that Hannibal had done to Italy in the earlier war. In 149 BC Rome declared war on Carthage again on spurious pretexts, initiating the Third Punic War. The Carthaginians attempted to negotiate a settlement, but the Romans would be satisfied with nothing less than the city's total destruction. After a long siege, the city fell and its people were killed or sold into slavery. Carthage itself was pulled apart brick by brick and completely destroyed. The city would be rebuilt by the Romans, but the former Phoenician state would never rise again.</English>
 	</TEXT>
 
 
